  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nRain from late today through Wednesday morning. Some drought\nrelief is possible across Northern Maine.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA backdoor cold front ahead of the approaching warm front will\nbring the initial rain showers tonight and into Tuesday. The NE\nflow begins late tonight as the warm front pushes in moisture.\nBy Tuesday, the NE flow will become more variable once the warm\nfront moves in. Rain amounts range from 0.75 inches to close to\n1 inch in the St John Valley, around a half inch in Aroostook \nand North Woods, and less than a half inch through the Central \nHighlands. Further south can expect less amounts with more \nintermittent rain.\n\nOn Wednesday: A northern stream shortwave passes to the north \nover the top of deep layered ridging building over New England. \nAt the surface and low levels, a warm front lifts northeast \nacross Maine. The result should be steady rain mainly across the\nNorth Wednesday morning, possibly lingering into the afternoon \nacross far NE Aroostook County. The main forecast challenge for \nWednesday is how quickly the warm front lifts to the northeast. \nThe NBM remains on the warm side across the North, noting its \nqpf and sky cover - so have under cut it for highs here, but \nacross Downeast Maine and the Bangor region, raised highs a bit \nto reflect a morning passage of the warm front there. Also used \nRFC QPF to reflect slightly higher QPF across the north.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nPotential for significant rain at some point from Thursday \nnight into early next week, but confidence is still fairly low \nin timing and amounts. However, confidence is increasing in some\ndrought relief during this time frame.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nAt least three closed lows are going to influence the regions\nweather from Thursday night into early next week. Unfortunately,\nclosed lows are among the things that models handle least well,\nso there is above normal uncertainty in the details during this\ntime frame, in particular the exact timing, strength and\nlocation of these systems. However, there is sufficient\ncommonality in deterministic and ensemble solutions, that there\nis increasing confidence in a significant rainfall at some \npoint from Thursday night into early next week.\n\nIn general the first closed upper low passes to the north \nThursday night and Friday, The second closed low slowly tracks\nfrom the Great Lakes to southern Quebec Friday night through\nSaturday night, then passes near or to the north of the region\nSunday and Sunday night. The third closed low then approaches\nfrom the west on Monday. What is the most uncertain is how much\ninteraction these three systems have with each other. In general\nthe more interaction, the more rainfall, the less interaction,\nthen lower amounts of rainfall. For now the chance for at least\n1\" of rain in this time frame ranges from 45% across far NW\nMaine to around 75% in coastal Washington County. The chance for\nat least 3\" of rain in this time frame ranges from around 10%\nacross N Penobscot/SE Aroostook to around 40% along the\nDowneast Coast. Based on the increasing probabilities, there is\nincreasing confidence in some drought relief from rainfall late\nthis week/early next week.\n\nAt this time, the most significant winds from Thursday night\ninto early next week are expected offshore, with wind gusts over\nland, outside of higher elevations, likely 35 mph or less.",
